# What is the universe made of?
subtitle: "Dark energy, dark matter, and everything we can see: guess how big a slice of the universe is ordinary matter, and see how little we know."

## What is the universe made of?
text: {"font_size":34}
use: pie_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Dark energy
      - Dark matter
      - Ordinary matter
    values:
      - 68
      - 27
      - 5
    box:
      x: 100
      'y': 140
      w: 800
      h: 500
    percent: false
sources:
    - id: planck
      title: Planck 2018 results. VI. Cosmological parameters
      authors: Planck Collaboration
      year: 2020
      doi: 10.1051/0004-6361/201833910
      finding: about 68% dark energy, 27% dark matter and 5% ordinary matter
    - id: sn
      title: The Nobel Prize in Physics 2011
      authors: Nobel Foundation
      year: 2011
      finding: 'Perlmutter, Schmidt and Riess: distant supernovae in 1998 showed the expansion of the universe is accelerating'

    text p68 "68%" #2f2b26 cites ["planck"] x 610 y 330 font_size 56 hidden true
    text p27 "27%" #2f2b26 cites ["planck"] x 385 y 445 font_size 52 hidden true

    card title "What is the universe made of?"

What is the universe made of? The biggest share is dark energy, which makes space expand faster and faster.
    draw slice_1

Next comes dark matter: unseen stuff whose gravity holds the galaxies together.
    draw slice_2

    ask "And everything else, every star, planet and person: how big a share is that? Drag its edge." on slice_3 store g default "20"
        right: You said {g}; it is {g.true}: all the ordinary matter is about one twentieth.
        wrong: You said {g}. It is only {g.true}: about one twentieth of everything.

Every atom on the periodic table fits in that thin wedge: every star, every planet, and you.
    text p5 "5%" #2f6b8f cites ["planck"] x 565 y 628 font_size 52

Dark matter is about five times as much as all of that.
    draw p27

We know it is there because galaxies spin too fast to hold together on the gravity of what we can see.
    highlight slice_2

And dark energy is about two thirds of everything.
    draw p68

It turned up in 1998, when distant exploding stars looked dimmer than expected: the expansion is speeding up.
    highlight slice_1

The shares come from the Planck satellite, which mapped the oldest light in the universe.
    highlight slice_1 slice_2

So about ninety-five percent of the universe is something nobody has yet identified.
    text unknown "95% unknown" #b5482e cites ["planck"] x 810 y 560 font_size 48
